detail:
MEASURED with karac built at 555c29b44e (main, 2026-10-14; release for `check` and `__mir-run`, debug llvm for `__mir-llvm-run` and `__mir-llvm-asan`):

    struct Node {
        val: i64,
        children: Vec[Node],
    }

    fn first_then_pop(t: Node) -> i64 {
        let mut v: Vec[(ref Node, i64)] = Vec.new();
        for c in t.children.iter() {
            v.push((c, 0));
        }
        let (a, n) = v[0];
        v.pop();
        a.val + n
    }

    fn main() {
        let t = Node { val: 1, children: vec![Node { val: 2, children: Vec.new() }, Node { val: 3, children: Vec.new() }] };
        println(f"{first_then_pop(t)}");
    }

`karac check` passes it. `karac __mir-run`, `karac __mir-llvm-run` and `karac __mir-llvm-asan` all stop with "error[mir]: borrow check first_then_pop: bb9[5]: mutable borrow of _2 while it is borrowed (borrow at bb4[1]) [E0516]". The expected output is `2`.

Variations, each on both MIR backends:
- `let a = v[0].0; let n = v[0].1;` in place of the destructuring: the same refusal.
- `let pair = v[0]; let a = pair.0; let n = pair.1;`: the same refusal, so the borrow follows the copied tuple too.
- the destructuring, with `a.val` read into a local BEFORE `v.pop()` and only that local used after: runs, prints `2`. So the refusal is about `a` being live across the mutation.
- the same shape over `Vec[ref Node]`, `let a = v[0]; v.pop(); a.val`: runs, prints `2` (that `karac check` half was B-2026-10-14-10, fixed in 8139c5e826).
- `Vec[(i64, i64)]` with `let (a, n) = v[0]; v.pop();`: runs.

WHY IT IS A FALSE POSITIVE: core-semantics §1.1 lists `ref T` (not `mut ref T`) and tuples whose parts are `Copy` as `Copy`, and says "Copying a shared reference gives a second reference to the same place, with the same origins (§5.3)". The `ref Node` held in the vector's element borrows the tree `t`, so the copy taken out of `v[0]` borrows `t` as well; reading the element borrows `v` only for the copy. Mutating `v` afterwards conflicts with nothing the program still uses. The `Vec[ref Node]` case already gets this right; the tuple case keeps the vector's borrow alive through the copied reference. Rust accepts the same program (`let (a, n) = v[0];` on a `Vec<(&Node, i64)>`, then `v.pop()`).

SOURCE NOTE: found writing kata 589 (N-ary Tree Preorder Traversal), whose frames arm keeps the recursion's frames by hand as a `Vec[(ref Node, i64)]`, a node and the index of its next child, reading the top frame with `let (node, next) = frames[top];` and then popping it or bumping its index. Two stacks side by side, a `Vec[ref Node]` and a `Vec[i64]`, run on both backends.

Measured on main b5ca6661bc, `karac check` and `karac __mir-run`.

1. The impl the spec shows is refused:

    struct Bag { items: Vec[i64] }
    impl IntoIterator for Bag {
        type Item = i64;
        fn into_iter(own self) -> impl Iterator[Item = i64] { self.items.into_iter() }
    }

   check: `E0203 impl of trait 'IntoIterator' is missing associated type 'IntoIter'`.

2. With `type IntoIter = VecIntoIter[i64];` added (accepted), a generic consumer still cannot reach the method, because the trait declares none:

    fn total[C: IntoIterator[Item = i64]](c: own C) -> i64 {
        let mut s = 0;
        for x in c.into_iter() { s += x; }
        s
    }

   check: `no method 'into_iter' on type parameter 'C'; add a trait bound declaring it`. This is the generic-library shape (a function taking any consumable collection) that docs/library uses for `Vec.extend`, `collect` sources and friends.

3. Still with the `IntoIter` line, `let mut v: Vec[i64] = Vec.new(); v.extend(Bag { items: vec![3, 4] });` passes `karac check` (B-2026-10-13-19 special-cased `extend` in src/typechecker/method_vec_mutation.rs rather than going through the trait), and `__mir-run` then refuses it: `error[mir]: build: 2286:25 in vec_extend_iter[i64, Bag]: the method call .next with no resolved callee is not lowered yet`. So check passes a program nothing runs. A direct `b.into_iter().count()` on the same Bag runs (prints 2): the impl's method is reachable by name on the concrete type, just not through the trait.

SOURCE NOTE: found while probing kata 811 (Subdomain Visit Count); its iterator arm's `flat_map` closure returns a `Vec`, which needs `.into_iter()` (flat_map's `U: Iterator` is the spec's, docs/library/iterators.md), and the probe went on to the trait itself. The kata needed no workaround: `Vec.into_iter()` is inherent. Owner: Thread A (stdlib in Kāra: iterators).

Measured on main 477e314d59 (release `karac`), `karac check` and `karac __mir-run`:

    fn main() {
        let n = 4;
        let pairs: Vec[i64] = (0..n).flat_map(|i| (i + 1..n).map(|j| i * 10 + j)).collect();
        println(f"{pairs}");
    }

check: `E0509 the result borrows i, which this function owns, so it would outlive it (core-semantics.md §5.4)` at the inner range. __mir-run: `borrow check main.c0: bb4[term]: the result borrows _2, which this function owns [E0509]`. With the captured value bound to a local first, `flat_map(|i| { let base = i * 10; (i + 1..n).map(|j| base + j) })`, both report E0516 (`cannot end base while it is still borrowed`) and the MIR one adds E0509. Expected `[1, 2, 3, 12, 13, 23]`.

The spec: §9.1 says a capture whose place dies while the closure is still live is by move instead, whatever the type, and a `Copy` place is copied; `i` (and `base`) die when the outer closure's call returns, while the inner closure lives on inside the returned `IterMap`. §9.3 independently makes a closure inside a returned value escaping, which captures every place by move. Rust's spelling is `move |j|`; design.md's table of rejected keywords sends `move` to E_MOVE_NOT_USED, so a Kāra user has no way to ask for the copy and the canonical nested `flat_map` for pairs and triples cannot be written.

SOURCE NOTE: kata 812 (Largest Triangle Area). Its iterator arm was first written `(0..n).flat_map(|i| (i + 1..n).flat_map(|j| (j + 1..n).map(|k| twice_area(points[i], points[j], points[k])))).max()`; it now nests `map(..).max()` per corner instead, whose inner closures do not outlive their enclosing call, and that is right on every v2 surface. Owner: Thread A (ownership in `karac check`), with Thread B / drop schedule for the MIR borrowck half.

KATA 816 (Ambiguous Coordinates, 2026-10-20), same release `karac` (f6f2efb14): the NON-`Copy` half of §9.1 is refused the same way. The natural iterator arm pairs every left number with every right one:

    fn pairs(digits: Str) -> Vec[String] {
        (1..digits.len())
            .flat_map(|cut| {
                parts(digits[0..cut]).into_iter().flat_map(|x| parts(digits[cut..digits.len()]).into_iter().map(|y| f"({x}, {y})"))
            })
            .collect()
    }

`karac check`: E0509 `the result borrows cut` (the `Copy` case above) and, for the `String` parameter `x` of the middle closure, E0516 `cannot drop x while it is still borrowed` plus E0509 `the result borrows x`. §9.1 says a place that dies while the closure is live is moved in 'whatever the type', and a non-`Copy` one is moved, so `x` should move into the inner `map` closure. (Writing `rights.iter().map(..)` over a `rights` computed once per cut is a different question, a closure returning a view of its own capture, and is not part of this row.) The kata's iterator arm is one `filter_map` over (cut, point, point) triples instead.

SAME RULE THROUGH A FUNCTION RETURN (Thread A, 2026-10-22, main 7a0a10621d): `fn adder(i: i64) -> impl Iterator[Item = i64] { (i + 1..4).map(|j| i * 10 + j) }` with `let v: Vec[i64] = adder(1).collect();` is refused by `__mir-run` with `borrow check adder: ... the result borrows _1, which this function owns [E0509]` (with the value first bound to a local, E0516 plus E0509). §9.3 lists a returned value as one way a closure escapes, so the capture is by move. Since B-2026-10-20-35 closed, this is the only thing in the way of that shape.

Repro (MIR pipeline, karac check / karac fix at origin/main):

#[derive(Clone)]
struct NDef { bold: bool }
fn main() {
    let mut nd: Map[String, NDef] = Map.new();
    nd.insert("".to_string(), NDef { bold: false });
    let parent = nd.get("");
    let flag = true;
    let mut n = match parent {
        None => NDef { bold: false },
        Some(p) => {
            if flag { nd.get("").unwrap() } else { p }
        }
    };
    n.bold = true;
    println(f"{n.bold}");
}

`karac check` reports three errors: a typecheck one at the `Some(p) =>` arm (13:20, 'cannot move a non-Copy value out of a borrowed place ... Write `.clone()`') and two borrow_projection_copy ones at the inner tails `nd.get("").unwrap()` and `p`. `karac fix` applies the two inner fixes and prints `warning: skipped a fix at f2.kara:19:10 (`.clone()`): applying it would not parse; please report it`: the arm-level fix would append `.clone()` after the arm's block. The fixed file checks clean, so the outcome is right, but the arm-level diagnostic duplicates the inner ones and its fix-it is unparseable. Expected: either no arm-level diagnostic when the inner tails carry their own fix-its, or a fix-it that parses (wrap the block, or point at the tails). Found writing mini_pygments' build_style (StyleMeta inheritance), where the same shape came up once in a 7900-line program.

SOURCE NOTE: kara-katas apps/cli/mini_pygments (f0b67e0).

Measured with the release build at c3f84545bb, `karac check` and `karac __mir-run` alike:

    fn main() {
        let text = "a b a";
        let mut m = Map.new();
        for w in text.split_whitespace() {
            let n = m.get(w) ?? 0;
            m[w.to_string()] = n + 1;
        }
        println(f"{m.len()}");
    }

check: `error[typecheck]: 6:11: expected 'StringSlice', found 'String'` at `w.to_string()`; `__mir-run`: `error[mir]: typecheck: 6:11: expected 'StringSlice', found 'String'`. The same with `if not m.contains_key(w) { m.insert(w.to_string(), 0); }`, and with `let mut seen = Set.new(); if not seen.contains(w) { seen.insert(w.to_string()); }`. Annotating the map, `let mut m: Map[String, i64] = Map.new();`, checks and runs, and so does `Set[String]`.

design.md § Type inference says a constructor whose parameters are unknown introduces a type variable that "any later use in the same function body may solve". Lookup by view (library/collections.md) makes `get`, `get_mut`, `contains`, `contains_key` and `remove` on a `String`-keyed map take a `Str`, so a `Str` lookup is consistent with `K = Str` and with `K = String`, and solving `K := Str` from it alone is a guess the insert then contradicts. Counting words with an unannotated map, lookup before insert, is the shape this hits, and the diagnostic points at the insert rather than at the lookup that fixed the type. What would fit the spec: leave `K` open on a view lookup (a `Str` argument constrains `K` to `Str` or `String`) and let the insert decide, or default to the owning type; failing that, a diagnostic at the lookup naming the annotation.

The message also names the internal `StringSlice` where the spec's type is `Str`.

