SOURCE NOTE: found writing apps/cli/mini_pyasn1 (kara-katas), whose first draft forwarded `w: mut ref W` unmarked in 477 places; it matched the oracle on __mir-run, then `karac check` refused it and `karac fix` added all 477 markers.

Repro (main, 2026-10-21):

    struct W { n: i64 }
    fn bump(w: mut ref W) { w.n += 1; }
    fn twice(w: mut ref W) { bump(w); bump(w); }
    fn main() {
        let mut w = W { n: 0 };
        twice(mut w);
        println(f"{w.n}");
    }

`karac check` and `karac build --mir`: error[typecheck] 3:31 and 3:40, "every argument to a mutating parameter is marked, a `mut ref` binding's included" (the rule from dfe519983b, B-2026-10-14-35). `karac __mir-run` and `karac __mir-llvm-run`: no error, print 2. The same MIR front ends DO enforce the sibling strict rule from B-2026-10-19-16: `fn set(x: mut ref i64) { x = 5; }` is refused by `__mir-run` with "plain `=` does not write through it". So the marker check is the one strict-check rule the MIR run front ends skip.

Why it matters: the corpus mir-interp, mir-llvm and mir-llvm-asan backends go through those front ends, so an entry missing markers passes three of the four backends and fails only mir-build, which reads as a build-only fault rather than a source that does not pass check. Expected: the MIR run front ends refuse it the way check and build do.

