SOURCE NOTE: found writing apps/cli/mini_webvtt (kara-katas): its argparse emulation walks `for (o, a) in option_strings()` and compared `*o == short`; `karac check` was clean and `__mir-run` then refused it. Owner: Thread B (MIR builder binding modes), with Thread A for `karac check`; the two must agree on the type of a destructured bare `for` element.

Repro (main, 2026-10-23):

    fn names() -> Vec[(String, i64)] {
        let mut v: Vec[(String, i64)] = Vec.new();
        v.push(("a".to_string(), 1));
        v
    }
    fn main() {
        let want = "a".to_string();
        let ns = names();
        for (o, n) in ns {
            if *o == want { println(f"{n}"); }
        }
    }

`karac check`: no diagnostic. `karac __mir-run`: error[mir] typecheck 10:12 "unary '*' requires 'ref T', 'mut ref T', or a raw pointer ... found 'String'". The same happens iterating the call `names()` directly. Without the `*` (`if o == want`) both accept it and it prints 1, so the kata drops the `*`. `karac check` elsewhere treats a bare `for` element as a borrow (its "cannot move 'line': it borrows its value (a bare `for` element ...)" diagnostic), so which side is right is a spec question for the owners; the divergence is the bug, because a program `karac check` passes should not be refused by `karac build --mir`.

SECOND SHAPE (2026-10-23, apps/cli/mini_pysubs2, whose format autodetection dedups `Vec[Option[String]]` guesses and whose `remove_miscellaneous_events` walks `for (a, b, t) in texts` over a `Vec[(i64, i64, String)]`). The binding need not come from a destructuring `for`; an `if let` over a bare `for` element does the same:

    fn main() {
        let mut all: Vec[Option[String]] = Vec.new();
        all.push(Some("a".to_string()));
        let t = "a".to_string();
        for g in all {
            if let Some(f) = g {
                if t == *f { println("eq"); }
            }
        }
        println(f"{all.len()}");
    }

`karac check`: no diagnostic. `karac __mir-run`: error[mir] typecheck 7:21 "unary '*' requires 'ref T' ... found 'String'". Without the `*` both accept it and print `eq` then `1`. Over an owned `Option[String]` (`if let Some(f) = o` with `o` a local) both sides refuse `*f`, so the divergence is in the binding mode of a pattern whose scrutinee is a bare `for` element. In the tuple shape the MIR side accepted `*a` on the `i64` field and refused only `*t` on the String one. The kata drops the `*` in both places.

THIRD SHAPE (2026-10-24, apps/cli/mini_pystrich, whose Data Matrix byte-by-byte path walks the segments of a borrowed payload). The element need not be destructured or matched with `if let`, and the binding need not be a String: a `match` on a bare `for` element over a FIELD of a borrowed struct does the same with an `i64` payload.

    enum Seg {
        Text(String),
        Code(i64),
    }
    struct Payload {
        segs: Vec[Seg],
    }
    fn total(p: ref Payload) -> i64 {
        let mut n: i64 = 0;
        for s in p.segs {
            match s {
                Seg.Code(v) => n += *v,
                Seg.Text(t) => n += t.len() as i64,
            }
        }
        n
    }
    fn main() {
        let p = Payload { segs: vec![Seg.Code(5), Seg.Text("abc".to_string())] };
        println(f"{total(p)}");
    }

`karac check`: no diagnostic. `karac run`, `__mir-run`, `__mir-llvm-run` and `build --mir`: error[mir] typecheck 14:33 "unary '*' requires 'ref T' ... found 'i64'; a 'i64' is copied, so drop the `*`". Without the `*` both sides accept it and print 8. The same function over a `segs: ref Vec[Seg]` parameter (`for s in segs`) is accepted with `*v` by both sides and prints 8, so here the divergence needs the field projection through the reference. The kata drops the `*` at that one site; three other `*v` sites in it, over a `ref Vec` parameter and over `t.c40[i]` with `t: ref DmTables`, run unchanged.

SOURCE NOTE: found writing apps/cli/mini_parsedatetime (kara-katas), whose reminders tool has a `todo` command that sorts data/todo.txt; the natural name for its function was `todo`. Owner: Thread A (karac check / typechecker builtin intercepts).

Repro (main, 2026-10-23):

    fn todo(a: i64, b: i64) -> i64 {
        a + b
    }
    fn main() {
        println(f"{todo(1, 2)}");
    }

`karac check`: error[typecheck] 5:16 "todo() takes 0 or 1 argument(s), found 2"; `karac __mir-run` refuses with the same message. The definition itself draws no diagnostic, so the program reads as if it calls its own function. design.md line 624: every file starts with `import std.prelude.*;` at the lowest precedence, "so any import or local name shadows a prelude name; a lint flags the likely-unintended cases". Likely cause (inferred, not bisected): the diverging-builtin intercept in src/typechecker/expr_call.rs (`name == "todo" || name == "unreachable" || name == "panic"` guarded only by `self.local_scope.lookup(name).is_none()`) checks local bindings but not the program's own top-level functions; the interpreter (eval_call.rs) and codegen (call_dispatch.rs) carry the same name list, so a fix has to reach all three.

Workaround in the kata: the function is named `todo_tool`, commented with this row's id.

WIDENED 2026-10-23 (apps/cli/mini_tinydb, kara-katas 415ce396): the same intercept catches `ref_eq`. src/typechecker/expr_call.rs's `ref_eq` intercept is guarded only by `self.local_scope.lookup("ref_eq").is_none()`, so a top-level `fn ref_eq(a: i64, b: i64, c: i64) -> bool` called as `ref_eq(1, 1, 1)` draws "`ref_eq` takes exactly two arguments, got 3" from `karac check` and `__mir-run`, and with two i64 arguments draws "`ref_eq` compares reference identity of `shared` values only". The port's structural-equality helper is named `obj_eq` instead, commented with this row's id.

fix:
bd015b5c7c (src/mir/lower.rs, the library-method argument loop): a stored argument whose type is a `ref` to a Copy value or handle used to be read through the borrow only when the argument was a place. A call result such as `m.get(k).unwrap()` or `a.get(i).unwrap()` now goes through `owned_operand` too, which also counts a stored handle. Before this, the reference itself went into the container: compiled code stored the element's address, and the MIR interpreter held a `Ref` that a later `<` refused. A container whose element type is a reference still stores the reference.

Fixture: tests/mir_build.rs `mir_a_stored_ref_a_call_returns_is_read_through` runs the row's repro (`1 0` / `less`) plus three more shapes, on __mir-run and compiled at -O0..-O3:
- `Vec.get(..).unwrap()` pushed;
- `Set.insert` and `Map.insert` of call results;
- a `shared` element pushed from `Vec.get(..).unwrap()`, which is the same object (mutating it shows through the original) and is ASan-clean under __mir-llvm-asan.

